# RCA: Ticket "Backspace occasionally hangs the desktop app in loading state" — Maude desktop app

> Ticket source: no tracker ID was given — a free-text bug report was pasted directly into `/flow:bug-rca` (`integrations.tracker.provider` is `github`, but the argument isn't an issue number, so this RCA treats it as a manually-pasted report per the command's fallback path).

Original report (Czech): "kdyz obcas zmacku v maude desktop app backspace na klavenici tak se hodi cela aplikace do loading state a uz to nenaskoci, nevim presne co to dela ale backspace by mel fungovat jen na delete elementu" — translation: sometimes pressing Backspace in the Maude desktop app throws the whole app into a loading state that never recovers; Backspace should only work to delete an element.

## Summary

The Tauri/WKWebView desktop shell has no native suppression of WebKit's default "Backspace = navigate back in history" gesture — the client-side `preventDefault()` in `apps/studio/client/app.jsx` is the *only* thing standing between a Backspace keypress and a full WKWebView back-navigation (which reloads the shell to "Starting…" and never recovers, since there's no meaningful prior navigation entry to resolve against). A shell-level guard for exactly this failure mode already exists (added in commit `578acc34`, 2026-07-07, "dogfood round 3 — Backspace crash"), but it has a gap: it returns *before* calling `preventDefault()` whenever there is no active canvas tab, or the active tab is the System tab — so Backspace pressed in either of those states (or during the brief window before a canvas has finished activating) still falls through to WebKit's default action and triggers the hang. This matches the "sometimes" in the report precisely.

## Root Cause

`apps/studio/client/app.jsx:9506-9525` — the shell-level Backspace/Delete guard:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  const onKey = (e) => {
    if (e.key !== 'Backspace' && e.key !== 'Delete') return;
    if (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.altKey) return;
    const t = e.target;
    const editable =
      t &&
      (t.tagName === 'INPUT' ||
        t.tagName === 'TEXTAREA' ||
        t.tagName === 'SELECT' ||
        t.isContentEditable);
    if (editable) return;
    if (!activePath || activePath === SYSTEM_TAB) return;   // <-- bails BEFORE preventDefault
    e.preventDefault();
    const one = Array.isArray(selected) ? (selected.length === 1 ? selected[0] : null) : selected;
    if (one?.artboardId && !one.id) deleteArtboardShell(one.artboardId);
  };
  window.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
}, [activePath, selected, deleteArtboardShell]);
```

Line 9518 is the bug: `if (!activePath || activePath === SYSTEM_TAB) return;` exits the handler — with no `preventDefault()` called — whenever:
- **No canvas tab is active yet** (`activePath` falsy) — e.g. right after launch, before the first canvas has finished activating, or after closing the last open tab.
- **The System tab is active** — the DS-management / settings surface, which is not backed by a canvas iframe.

In both states, focus is very likely to sit on `document.body` or some non-input shell chrome element (nothing in the System tab or the empty-state UI grabs/needs focus), so `editable` is `false` and control reaches line 9518, which then returns without suppressing the default action. WebKit's native "Backspace = back" gesture then fires, and because the desktop shell has no `decidePolicyForNavigationAction`-equivalent interception in the Rust layer (`apps/desktop/src-tauri/` — confirmed no `allowsBackForwardNavigationGestures`, no navigation-delegate, no history handling anywhere in source) and no meaningful prior navigation entry to resolve against, the WKWebView reloads to the app's initial "Starting…" loading screen and never recovers.

This is a genuine regression gap in an existing, deliberate fix — not a new bug class. The comment directly above the guard (lines 9497–9505) documents the exact failure mode this ticket reports, confirming the shell author was already aware of the WKWebView back-nav risk, but the fix's scope (activePath / SYSTEM_TAB early-return) didn't cover every focus state where a stray Backspace can occur.

Other Backspace/Delete handlers in the codebase were checked and ruled out as contributing causes:
- `apps/studio/annotations-layer.tsx:3011` — capture-phase `document` listener, but gated behind an active annotation selection before any `preventDefault()`; correctly falls through to the shell guard when nothing is selected (i.e., it relies on the guard above, but doesn't itself cause the bug).
- `apps/studio/client/app.jsx:4804` — a narrowly-scoped `onKeyDown` on a small provenance-reset button; not global, not implicated.
- `apps/studio/input-router.tsx`, `apps/studio/use-annotation-resize.tsx` — no Backspace/Delete handling.

No DDR documents this WKWebView-specific gotcha (searched `.ai/archive/decisions/*.md` for "WKWebView"/"backspace"/"navigation"/"history" — only tangential hits in the native-shell architecture DDRs).

## Impact

- **Who:** Every desktop-app (Tauri/WKWebView) user. Web-mode users are unaffected (there's no native-shell "back gesture" concern on the web build, or the back gesture doesn't destroy app state the same way).
- **Severity:** High for desktop users when triggered — the app becomes fully unusable (permanent "Starting…" spinner) and requires a manual relaunch, with risk of losing unsaved in-memory state (e.g. an in-progress edit not yet flushed to disk).
- **Trigger frequency:** "Sometimes" — specifically whenever Backspace is pressed while (a) no canvas tab is open/active yet, or (b) the System tab is focused, and focus is not inside a text input. Users hitting Backbackspace out of habit (e.g. after a delete, or while nothing is selected) while browsing the System tab or between tab switches will hit this.
- **Workaround:** Avoid pressing Backspace while on the System tab or before a canvas has loaded; if triggered, relaunch the app.

## Proposed Fix

Move `e.preventDefault()` above the `activePath`/`SYSTEM_TAB` early-return (line 9518) so the WKWebView back-navigation is suppressed unconditionally once we know focus isn't in an editable field — independent of which tab is active or whether a canvas has loaded yet. The artboard-delete side-effect (`deleteArtboardShell`) can stay conditional on `activePath`/`selected`, since that's app logic, not default-action suppression:

```js
if (editable) return;
e.preventDefault();                                  // unconditional, once we know focus isn't editable
if (!activePath || activePath === SYSTEM_TAB) return; // logic-only early-return now, no default-action leak
const one = Array.isArray(selected) ? (selected.length === 1 ? selected[0] : null) : selected;
if (one?.artboardId && !one.id) deleteArtboardShell(one.artboardId);
```

Files to change:
- `apps/studio/client/app.jsx` (~line 9506-9525) — reorder as above.

Given the native-layer fragility this bug exposes (client-JS `preventDefault()` is the *only* backstop against a destructive WKWebView default action, with zero redundancy in the Rust shell), also consider — as a defense-in-depth follow-up, not required for this fix — adding a native-side guard in `apps/desktop/src-tauri/` (e.g. disabling `allowsBackForwardNavigationGestures` / intercepting back-navigation at the WebView delegate level) so a future client-JS regression can't reproduce the same crash class. Worth a DDR if pursued, since none currently documents this.

## Testing Requirements

- Manual/desktop-E2E (`apps/desktop/e2e/`, DOM-driven per project convention — no computer-use): a new scenario that (1) launches the app with no canvas open, presses Backspace, asserts the app is still responsive (not stuck on the "Starting…" screen); (2) switches to the System tab, presses Backspace, asserts the same; (3) selects a single artboard on an active canvas, presses Backspace, asserts the artboard is deleted (regression guard for the existing intended behavior); (4) selects a canvas element (focus inside canvas iframe), presses Backspace, asserts the element is deleted and the shell-level handler didn't double-handle it.
- No unit-testable surface for the WKWebView default-action suppression itself (it's a DOM-event/native-webview interaction) — desktop E2E is the only meaningful verification layer here, consistent with `feedback_prefer_dom_driven_e2e_not_computer_use`.

## Complexity

Low — a two-line reorder in one `useEffect`. The desktop-E2E scenario authoring is the larger portion of the work.

# RCA: Ticket "check for updates dělá nic" — Maude ▸ Check for Updates… silently no-ops when an update IS found but fails to install

## Summary

User has Maude Desktop 0.45.2 installed; 0.46.0 is out. Clicking **Maude ▸ Check for Updates…** does nothing — no "you're up to date" dialog, no error, no update banner. The update feed (`https://maude.sh/releases/{target}/{arch}/{current_version}`) was verified live and correctly returns a `0.46.0` payload (version, notes, signed `.app.tar.gz` URL) for `current_version=0.45.2` — so the server side is healthy. The bug is client-side: `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/updater.rs::check_and_apply` has an error-handling asymmetry. Two of its three outcome branches (`Ok(None)` "already current", top-level `Err` "check failed") show a native dialog when the user triggered the check interactively. The third branch — `Ok(Some(update))` where an update IS found but `update.download_and_install(...)` subsequently fails — only does `eprintln!` to stderr, which is invisible in a packaged `.app` with no attached terminal. Since a real update always exists in this report (0.46.0 > 0.45.2), the "up to date" dialog is correctly never shown, and if the download/install step fails for any reason, the user experiences exactly the reported symptom: click → nothing.

## Root Cause

`apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/updater.rs`, function `check_and_apply` (lines 79–136):

```rust
match updater.check().await {
    Ok(Some(update)) => {
        ...
        let result = update.download_and_install(...).await;
        match result {
            Ok(()) => { /* emits `update-ready`, client shows a banner */ }
            Err(e) => eprintln!("[maude] update download/install failed: {e}"),   // <-- silent
        }
    }
    Ok(None) => {
        if interactive { /* shows "You're up to date" dialog */ }               // <-- has feedback
    }
    Err(e) => {
        if interactive { notify_check_failed(handle, &e.to_string()); }         // <-- has feedback
    }
}
```

The `Err(e)` arm inside `Ok(Some(update))` never consults `interactive` and never calls `handle.dialog()...show(...)`. This asymmetry has existed since the update loop was first introduced (`188efc5a`, phase 26–32) and was not addressed when the manual "Check for Updates…" menu item + explicit dialog feedback was added later (`6482f770`) — that commit added dialogs for the other two branches but missed this one.

Confirmed via live probe: `curl https://maude.sh/releases/darwin/aarch64/0.45.2` → `200` with a valid `{version: "0.46.0", url, signature, ...}` payload, GitHub's `releases/latest` correctly resolves to the non-draft, non-prerelease `v0.46.0` release with a `Maude.app.tar.gz` + `.sig` asset pair. So `updater.check()` almost certainly returns `Ok(Some(update))` for this user — the failure has to be happening in the subsequent `download_and_install()` step (network hiccup fetching the ~170 MB asset, ed25519 signature re-verification, or — most likely for a `.app` not living in a writable location, e.g. still run from the mounted `.dmg` or a directory without write permission — the in-place bundle swap failing).

## Impact

- **Who**: any Desktop user who clicks the manual "Check for Updates…" menu item while an update genuinely exists AND the subsequent download/install step fails for any reason (network blip, permissions, disk space, running from a non-writable location).
- **Severity**: Medium — not data-destructive, but it's a confusing dead end: the user gets zero signal, can't tell whether the click registered, whether they're current, or whether something is broken, and has no actionable next step (they can't see the `eprintln!` without launching from a terminal).
- **Workaround**: download the new `.dmg`/`.msi`/`.deb` manually from `https://maude.sh` or the GitHub releases page and reinstall over the existing app.

## Proposed Fix

In `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/updater.rs`, surface the `download_and_install` failure the same way `notify_check_failed` already does for the other two branches:

```rust
Err(e) => {
    eprintln!("[maude] update download/install failed: {e}");
    if interactive {
        handle
            .dialog()
            .message(format!("Found Maude {version}, but couldn't install it.\n\n{e}"))
            .title("Update failed")
            .kind(MessageDialogKind::Error)
            .show(|_| {});
    }
}
```

(`version` needs to be captured before the `download_and_install` call consumes `update`, or cloned — it already is, at line 93.)

This restores the three-outcome symmetry (`found+installed` → banner, `already current` → dialog, `check failed` OR `found but couldn't install` → error dialog) for interactive checks, while background/focus checks (`interactive=false`) stay quiet as designed.

**Files to change:**
- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/updater.rs` — add the missing dialog call in the `Err(e)` arm of the `download_and_install` match.

## Testing Requirements

- Manual: on a dev build, force `download_and_install` to fail (e.g. point the updater endpoint at a release whose asset 404s, or run the app from a read-only mount) and confirm the "Update failed" dialog now appears on a manual "Check for Updates…" click.
- Manual: confirm the happy path (update found, installs cleanly) still shows the existing `update-ready` banner, unaffected by this change.
- No automated coverage exists for `updater.rs` (native Tauri Rust, outside the `apps/desktop/e2e` WebdriverIO DOM-driven harness's reach — it only drives the WKWebView, not native dialogs). Note this gap; consider it out of scope for the fix itself.

## Complexity

Low — a few lines in one function, no schema/IPC/menu changes needed.
